1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Hello everyone, in this video we are going to see about impedance and reactance of capacitors

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Let's see the first definition

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:14,000
The flow of AC current in a capacitor is limited or restricted by internal impedance of capacitor

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Also known as capacitive reactance

5
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
When we apply AC current to a capacitor, the capacitor will not allow all the current applied through it

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
It will restrict the current coming through it

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
This is called capacitive reactance

8
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The symbol of capacitive reactance is Xe

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Its unit is Ohms

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
The formula for calculating capacitive reactance is

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Xe is equal to 1 divided by 2 pi Fc

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Xe is the capacitive reactance measured in Ohms

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
F is the applied frequency

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
C is the capacitance of capacitor

15
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Capacitive reactance is the resistance inside the capacitor

16
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Capacitive reactance is the resistance inside the capacitor

17
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:01,000
But Capacitive reactance doesn't have any constant

18
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Capacitive reactance is change because of bullied resistance

19
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
I.e. Capacitive reactance is not a constant

20
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The resistance is getting diminished by applying resistance

21
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Take an example of a circuit

22
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The capacitor is present in AC supply

23
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Let's check the graph

24
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:26,000
The resistance of capacitor is being changed by applying resistance

25
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:27,000
In this graph

26
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Let's check the graph

27
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,599
we have taken frequency in x-axis and capacitive reactance in y-axis

28
00:01:32,599 --> 00:01:35,900
so initially when the frequency is low

29
00:01:35,900 --> 00:01:38,900
the capacitive reactance will be very high

30
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:43,200
when we increase the frequency, the reactance value will decrease

31
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:49,200
that is, the AC frequency applied across the capacitance is 0 Hz

32
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:52,200
that is, if we give 0 Hz to the capacitance

33
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:55,200
we can see in the formula, x is equal to 1 by 2 pi fc

34
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:58,200
if the frequency is 0, 1 by 0 is infinity

35
00:01:58,200 --> 00:02:02,200
so the capacitance of the capacitor will be very high

36
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:05,200
if the resistance is high, the current will be 0

37
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:08,199
if the frequency is infinity, if we substitute in the formula

38
00:02:08,199 --> 00:02:11,199
1 by 2 pi infinity into c

39
00:02:11,199 --> 00:02:14,199
1 by infinity will be 0

40
00:02:14,199 --> 00:02:16,199
so the capacitive reactance is 0

41
00:02:16,199 --> 00:02:19,199
if we see how it behaves, it will behave like a short circuit

42
00:02:19,199 --> 00:02:21,199
so the current will flow maximally

43
00:02:21,199 --> 00:02:24,199
next, let us see the example of the reactance of capacitor

44
00:02:24,199 --> 00:02:25,199
here, I have taken 2 capacitors

45
00:02:25,199 --> 00:02:27,199
I have taken 229 F capacitor

46
00:02:27,199 --> 00:02:30,199
when applying 1 kHz frequency and 20 kHz frequency for that

47
00:02:30,199 --> 00:02:35,199
we are going to see the value of the capacitive reactance across the capacitor

48
00:02:35,199 --> 00:02:37,199
so in the image on the left side

49
00:02:37,199 --> 00:02:39,199
229 F is equal to 1 kHz

50
00:02:39,199 --> 00:02:43,199
on the right side, the same 229 F capacitor is equal to 20 kHz

51
00:02:43,199 --> 00:02:46,199
we know the formula for calculating the reactance of capacitor

52
00:02:46,199 --> 00:02:48,199
x is equal to 1 by 2 pi fc

53
00:02:48,199 --> 00:02:51,199
1 by 2 pi into f is 1 kHz

54
00:02:51,199 --> 00:02:54,199
into c is 229 F

55
00:02:54,199 --> 00:02:57,199
to calculate this, we get 723.4 ohms

56
00:02:57,199 --> 00:03:01,199
so the value of the capacitive reactance is 723.4 ohms

57
00:03:01,199 --> 00:03:03,199
that is, the resistance of the capacitor

58
00:03:03,199 --> 00:03:07,199
now, let us calculate the capacitive reactance of the circuit on the right side

59
00:03:07,199 --> 00:03:10,199
in the same formula, 1 by 2 pi f

60
00:03:10,199 --> 00:03:13,199
the frequency value is 20 kHz

61
00:03:13,199 --> 00:03:15,199
to solve this, we get 36.2 ohms

62
00:03:15,199 --> 00:03:20,199
that is, the current reactance of the capacitor is 36.2 ohms

63
00:03:20,199 --> 00:03:22,199
from this, we can confirm that

64
00:03:22,199 --> 00:03:24,199
when the frequency is less

65
00:03:24,199 --> 00:03:29,199
the resistance across the capacitor will be more

66
00:03:29,199 --> 00:03:31,199
when the frequency is more

67
00:03:31,199 --> 00:03:36,199
the resistance across the capacitor will be less

68
00:03:36,199 --> 00:03:37,199
thank you

